  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1035 PM MDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nUpper level high pressure will continue to control our weather\nthrough the end of the week, but then we will see a pattern\nchange. On Wednesday the upper level ridge will begin to drift to\nthe west. We will still have a hot day on Wednesday, but not quite\nas hot as Tuesday. The lower Rio Grande Valley below El Paso,\nlooks like they will be hot enough to need a heat advisory, but \nmuch of the lowlands will fall just short of heat advisory \ncriteria (105F). Without the upper level ridge sitting directly \nover southern New Mexico, the mountains will be able to fire off a\nfew showers and thunderstorms. For Thursday the upper level ridge\nwill basically be in the same position over the New \nMexico/Arizona border, but some moisture will push into the region\nfrom the east. This moisture will help fuel scattered mountain \nand lowland thunderstorms Thursday afternoon and evening. By \nFriday the upper level ridge will be back on the move again and it\nwill be centered over central New Mexico. Rain chances will again\nbe limited to area mountains and locations west of Rio Grande. \nHigh temperatures for Thursday and Friday will drop a few more \ndegrees so that by Friday afternoon most lowlands locations will \nsee high temperatures a little below 100F. \n\nFor Saturday and Sunday the upper level ridge will be well to our\neast which will set up a decent monsoon flow pattern.\nUnfortunately almost all of the moisture will be pushed into\nArizona. Rainfall both days will be limited to area mountains and\nlocations along the New Mexico and Arizona border. High\ntemperatures both days will run 4 to 8 degrees above average.\n\nFor the start of the week, an approaching West Coast trough\n(haven't talked about one of those in several months), will push\nsome of that monsoon moisture back to the east, so we will see a\nchance for afternoon and evening thunderstorms Monday and Tuesday\nfor much of the area. High temperatures in the lowlands both days\nwill run at or a little below the triple digit mark.",
